## Your slots

1. **pillars + chapter_titles** — choose {{ skeleton.min_chapters }}..{{ skeleton.max_chapters }}
   chapter headings cited from heading lines (h1/h2/h3): the things the site
   itself leads with. Each becomes a chapter; their ORDER is your narrative.
   Then write exactly ONE `chapter_titles` entry per cited pillar, in the
   same order: a complete, concise display title of at most 20 characters,
   faithfully condensing that pillar. Never return a truncated heading,
   unfinished clause or ellipsis; the citation keeps the full source fact,
   the title is the short display copy.
2. **hero** — `hero_word`: the product's name as the site writes it (cite a
   title/h1 line); `hero_sub`: the site's tagline (cite).
3. **cold_open** — 1..3 short hook lines, FREE TEXT (your creative channel):
   at most 3 words each (CJK: at most 8 characters). A hook is the film's
   own framing — rhythm, a question, a tease. It is NEVER a product claim:
   no features, numbers, customers or promises the page does not state —
   anything factual must be cited, and this slot cannot cite.
4. **reveal** — a content-specific surprise beat: ALWAYS write `reveal_lead`
   as a short, fresh lead-in suited to this film's tone and language. It is
   FREE TEXT and non-factual (a transition, question or tease), not a product
   claim. Do not leave it null and do not default to a stock catchphrase.
   `reveal_word` (1-2 words) and `reveal_sub` (one line) are cited — something
   true the site states but does not lead with (a free tier, a number, an
   integration).
5. **end** — `end_name` (the product's name) and `end_tagline`, both cited.
6. **narration** — spoken voiceover, keyed by scene key: one or two natural
   sentences per scene, ≤ 13 latin chars per second of the scene's window
   (CJK: ≈3 glyphs per second — each glyph speaks a syllable). Speech is your
   creative
   channel too: rephrase the page's facts into natural spoken language —
   TRUE to the manifest (never invent a claim), in the FILM's language (the
   pinned one above, else the PAGE's own — never the conversation's). Skip
   scenes that play better silent (a divider beat, the fast cold open);
   narrate the scenes where speech carries the story (hero, walks, reveal,
   end). A line too long for its scene is REJECTED — shorten it or leave
   the scene silent; the window never stretches for the words. Narration is
   spoken audio only; visible subtitles are a separate channel below.
